they were called comfort women a term  used to describe women and girls forced  to work in brothels run by the japanese  military  it's estimated up to 200 000 girls and  women from asia were sexually enslaved  they endured rapes beatings even murder  those who survived often had to live  with the stigma  a significant number were from korea a  former colony of japan  and it's here in south korea that the  world's longest running protest  continues to take place  each wednesday around noon  survivors activists and ordinary  citizens have gathered in front of the  former japanese embassy in seoul to  demand an apology and compensation from  the japanese government  this year some survivors sent video  messages as covet 19 concerns prevented  them from attending in person  japan insists that it never forcibly  dragged us out of korea and never made  us suffer but i demand they stop lying  and be honest  there are only 13 of the women still  alive in south korea  grandmothers are passing away without  receiving a proper apology and  compensation let's fight until the  japanese government gives an official  apology and legal reparations are made  so that the lives and cries of the  grandmothers are not in vain  six years ago japan and south korea  reached an agreement  japan apologized and provided nearly  nine million dollars to a fund to help  survivors but that deal has largely  fallen through  survivors were critical of it saying it  prioritized money over a more sincere  apology from japan  and when president moon jae-in took  office he effectively abandoned the  agreement signed by his predecessor  calling it seriously flawed  relations between south korea and japan  took a further dive yet week after week  year after year  protesters remain undeterred in their  quest for justice  as the demonstrations continue into a  third decade  a new generation of activists have taken  up the fight  florence louis al jazeera
 

